Ritemark Store Compliance: no external software acquisition promotion
Sprint 126 (R4): Microsoft Store certification finding 10.1.5 — Source Control
offered "Download Git for Windows" linking to git-scm.com. VS Code renders a
welcome-view line as a full-width primary Button when the line parses to
exactly one link and nothing else (viewPane.ts), so the flagged element was a
large blue call-to-action.

Ritemark now informs without offering: extensions/git/package.nls.json's
three scm.missing.* strings lose their bare download-link line. No line is
left that parses to a single link, so nothing renders as a button. The
explanation and the reload / troubleshoot commands stay. All three are gated
on `git.missing`, so an installed Git is untouched by construction.

Sprint 133 (R12): the Getting Started page's launch check, whose Git and Node
lines this patch also stripped of "Click here to install", retired with the
page. Its rows live on in the welcome and in Ritemark Settings, still without
any download or install link.

Note: Visual Studio Code ships in the Microsoft Store with the same button,
so this is not evidence that mentioning Git acquisition is categorically
forbidden. The resubmission describes what changed, not a policy reading.
Sprint 135: carried onto VS Code 1.142.0 unchanged.
Sprint 146 (R7, #567; the Experience lead's words: ritemark-dev releases/v2.2.0/analysis/
2026-10-31-sprint-146-experience-answers.md, section 5): File changes speaks Ritemark where there is no Git. Every Git
welcome string the panel shows (view.workbench.scm.*: no Git on the computer, Git turned off, no project open, no Git
in the project, a project with no folders, looking for Git, Git in a folder above, a folder Git doesn't trust, File
changes closed) is the lead's: a first line that is a sentence of its own, a second paragraph, and the same command as
before as its button ("Set up Git here" runs git.init, as Initialize Repository did). None says "VS Code",
"repository", "initialize", "source control" or "workspace", none links to docs, and "File changes needs Git" has no
button and no download link (still no line that parses to a lone link to software). GitHub's two Publish to GitHub
paragraphs leave the panel (extensions/github/package.json's viewsWelcome, decision 4); github.publish stays, so All
commands still offers Publish to GitHub. The explorer's Clone Repository and learn-more entries went in 003 already;
Git: Clone stays in All commands. The words live here because this patch owns the scm.missing lines they replace.
This replaces Sprint 126's wording above: the four scm.missing strings (the remote window's too) now hold the lead's
two lines, so its reload and troubleshoot links went with it. The welcome's Store rule stands as Sprint 126 set it.
Not here: the Git extension's own "Git not found" toast with its Download Git button (extensions/git/src/main.ts,
warnAboutMissingGit), a separate surface; the sprint's research/file-changes-audit.md names it and a one-line fix for it
(git.ignoreMissingGitWarning in product.json's configurationDefaults), not tracked by an issue when this was written.
Sprint 146 follow-ups (#634; the Experience lead's words, the same answers file, section 10 items 6 and the vscode.dev
link, on Jarmo's word of 2 November 2026): the pickers behind File changes' buttons speak Ritemark too
(extensions/git/src/commands.ts). Open it again (git.reopenClosedRepositories) is "Open File changes again" / "Choose a
folder", and with only one folder closed it opens that folder without asking. Allow this folder… (git.manageUnsafeRepositories)
is "Allow folders for Git" / "Choose the folders Git may use". Set up Git here in a project with several folders (git.init)
asks "Choose the folder to set up Git in", offers "Choose another folder…", and that folder dialog's button says "Set up Git
here". Both pickers' all-at-once row reads "All folders". GitHub's Copy vscode.dev Link leaves every menu: the six
github.copyVscodeDevLink* entries under file/share, editor/context/share, explorer/context/share, editor/lineNumber/context
and editor/title/context/share (extensions/github/package.json). A vscode.dev link opens the folder in VS Code's web
editor, not in Ritemark. Only the menu entries go: the commands and the share provider stay registered, and the commands
were already hidden from All commands upstream (commandPalette when false).
